                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Chronicles plays just like the first four games in the series, and by this point, nothing really new has been added to the formula, only refined. Visuals aren't all that dissimilar, the overall platforming feels about the same, and the sound design still follows the same minimalist principle. So gameplay-wise, if you liked the previous games, this one will also be a good one.\r\n\r\nIn a more overall look, the story itself will probably be a hit or miss. Since it follows directly after the ending of the previous game, the whole thing reads more like an interlude game while they prepare the plot for Lara's actual return from her presumed death. This means instead of one single story going through the entire game, we had 4 different ones all contained in their own 3 levels. This did lead to some interesting small changes, like inventories not carrying over between each chapter and, seeing how each one has only 3 levels to tell its tale, a lot more dialog and cutscenes spread through the gameplay. However, this also means there are no real climactic boss fights anywhere in the game, and even the endings on the final level can feel a bit anti-climactic once you finally get to it.\r\n\r\nThe game also plays and feels a lot shorter than the previous two games, and I honestly think it is a good thing. While the gameplay is a fun experience, it wasn't engaging enough for more than 20 hours, and after that point it began feeling like it was dragging. Chronicle's 12 hours, in contrast, feels a lot more well-paced and enjoyable for what it is.",
